D1/UHMzv0GZZZ3J6ibIiwpk4gUle4Orl/zJXegHzJH0HSYwuTNcibZZKvg\n6MRl//Dba3Wy3ZdlIU6CgSECWGxM6dZ13Gp+Z5v2nAhVftKnpDZVHewSvKi24Au3\ndc3+fM+bQclIwJOg4Jgr3qHVwTUflhcZpiP/xrMdModVS/Cn11ZIhEh7z/LhF5le\n6pschtcHpwbqB+vQRWy4lxli7fLlk5K8KWqtMLj7bmzA2qP+MtsUMvptr7zWCMIZ\nKoVsLJJ3iyvWgSNqaHinz7DAnO0xjRGGSnL5n9NTY9RiVrJ3PTeEIfnH67wR2/tD\nqf5Z/u3ypREwHzOTh5lYxIg5oEGfBm87nF0HcOnlwvRaWlr061nnz7SqlPafD4iu\n/zGHRrTkmkc8CyPfpMW0sOHMV45z1wXor9TMSL3Ddy6UXTX64pmazw7FT0tPvkss\n8TQEW+BeFEHGTNB7PlwjPMVjn9zB1dko6soV6Q31x8TC/o1yxb1LF0CjZqOuGlaI\nrk3gcAEOYv+G+sgO5/gR4706NVFHXpiUw3/Bw2mRQ/rlnGlvla1E2vb6/dAmbpxl\nGJBTprznXuz4LHGnoSWw\n=lkDr\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-24T11:03:55Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Avien/users-portal/commit/5a9b183ccb30c6b2cfb290533b98449609815633","node_id":"C_kwDORmGsMdoAKDVhOWIxODNjY2IzMGM2YjJjZmIyOTA1MzNiOTg0NDk2MDk4MTU2MzM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Avien/users-portal/commit/386ee308025d90866eec09dae52b046a1d169250","sha":"386ee308025d90866eec09dae52b046a1d169250","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Avien/users-portal/commits/386ee308025d90866eec09dae52b046a1d169250"}],"sha":"5a9b183ccb30c6b2cfb290533b98449609815633","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Avien/users-portal/commits/5a9b183ccb30c6b2cfb290533b98449609815633"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4569044?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/Avien","id":4569044,"login":"Avien","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1NjkwNDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Avien","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Avien/users-portal/commits/386ee308025d90866eec09dae52b046a1d169250/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-24T09:55:59Z","email":"avi.engelstein@gmail.com","name":"Avi Engelshtein"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-24T09:55:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(business-agent): product-facing AI agent (#12)\n\n* fix(business-agent): canonical Orders source-of-truth + move React host adapter to a lib\n\nFixes the Business Agent/UI divergence: the agent read static MOCK_ORDERS\nwhile the UI reflected live WS updates, so the two disagreed about current\nbusiness state. tools/orders-store.mjs is now the single canonical,\nin-memory, cross-connection order store — the origin for every order in\nthe system, including ones generated after process start.\n\n- tools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs: delegates to the store, broadcasts to\n  all WS clients (not just the connecting one), serves GET /api/orders\n  (plain Order[], for frontends) and GET /api/orders-snapshot (one atomic\n  {orders, arrivals} read, agent-only — avoids racing two separate HTTP\n  calls against a new order arriving in between) on the same port/process.\n  Startup demo-burst timers now fire once per process only once they\n  actually run (not once scheduled), fixing a StrictMode interaction where\n  an ephemeral first connection could silently consume the demo burst.\n- tools/business-agent-server.ts: fetchOrdersSnapshot() retries briefly\n  (absorbs the `concurrently` startup race) then fails loudly — no silent\n  fallback to stale static data. getUserOrders/getOrderMonitoringSignals\n  take the snapshot as a parameter instead of a static import.\n  getOrderMonitoringSignals gained real burst detection, reusing\n  isSecondOrderWithinBurstWindow/ORDER_BURST_WINDOW_MS unchanged.\n- tools/cors.mjs (new, shared): wildcard for local dev, a configurable\n  *_ALLOWED_ORIGINS env var for a real production allow-list later.\n- libs/users-react/data-access orders.api.ts: initial load now reads the\n  same canonical GET /api/orders instead of static MOCK_ORDERS, so a\n  browser refresh after WS orders arrived no longer resets to stale data.\n- use-orders-stream.ts / use-users-facade.ts: id-based dedup at both WS\n  merge points — the initial fetch and a WS message can now race and both\n  carry the same order, which the old buffering logic didn''t anticipate.\n- tools/orders-store.mjs: createOrdersStore() factory: one canonical\n  `ordersStore` singleton at runtime, fresh isolated instances in tests\n  (previously mutated shared module state across the suite).\n\nAlso moves the React host adapter for the Business Agent widget out of\nthe app and into libs/business-agent-react (type:feature, framework:react,\nmirrors libs/users-react/feature''s actual config shape) — consistent with\nthis repo''s convention that framework-specific code lives in libs/, not\napps/. The JSX custom-element typing for <business-agent-widget> is\nexposed through the library''s index.ts via a triple-slash reference so\nconsuming apps need no duplicate local .d.ts. The Business Agent panel is\nnow composed directly into the main Users/Orders page (app.tsx is the\ncomposition root; @portal/users-react/feature has no dependency on\n@portal/business-agent-react or vice versa) instead of living behind a\nseparate, undiscoverable /business-agent route.\n\nnpm run start:react now runs the WS mock, Business Agent server, and React\napp together via concurrently, with VITE_BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT wired for\nlocal dev.\n\nVerified end-to-end with the real Claude API and a real browser session:\nUI order count/total update live via WS, and Business Agent answers match\nthat same live state (not stale MOCK_ORDERS) for both a plain\n\"how many orders\" question (searchUsers -> getUserOrders) and a\nmonitoring question (searchUsers -> getOrderMonitoringSignals, correctly\nciting both the high-value and the new burst signal).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-react): restore demo loading latency + stabilize Business Agent layout\n\nTwo UX regressions surfaced by the canonical Orders source change:\n\n1. fetchOrdersByUser lost its ~800ms fake latency when it moved from static\n   MOCK_ORDERS to a real (near-instant) local fetch, so selecting a user no\n   longer visibly demonstrated the query loading state. Restored as optional\n   latency at the HTTP boundary instead of back in the data-access function:\n   tools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs''s GET /api/orders (never /api/orders-snapshot\n   — no reason to slow down the Business Agent) reads MOCK_ORDERS_API_DELAY_MS,\n   defaulting to 0. Railway''s production deployment always gets 0 (railway.json\n   invokes this file directly, bypassing package.json scripts entirely); the\n   800ms demo value is applied only inside start:react''s own mock:ws\n   invocation. TanStack Query caching semantics are untouched — this only\n   changes how long the underlying network response takes.\n\n2. On /users, Business Agent started at the viewport edge instead of the\n   dashboard''s column, and jumped vertically as order counts changed. Root\n   causes and fixes:\n   - Width: UserOrders'' own shellStyle (maxWidth/margin/padding) is now owned\n     by app.tsx''s UsersPage instead — the composition root wraps both\n     UserOrders and BusinessAgentPanel in one flex-column shell, so they share\n     exactly one column instead of each keeping an independently maintained\n     copy of the same constants. UserOrders no longer applies any shell style\n     to its own <section>.\n   - Height: OrdersCard sized its scroll container as\n     Math.min(orders.length, 8) * 52px, so the card visibly grew/shrank with\n     order count, pushing everything below it. Now always renders a fixed\n     416px viewport across all four states (loading/empty/error/list), with\n     the list scrolling inside it past 8 rows exactly as before.\n\nVerified live: dashboard title, Orders card, and Business Agent heading/input\nshare the same left pixel edge (zoomed screenshot); Business Agent''s vertical\nposition stayed identical switching Avi (5 orders) -> Dana (2 orders, through\nthe restored loading state) -> Noam (5 orders); a real Business Agent query\nafterward still answered correctly against the live state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): multi-turn conversation history + widget transcript\n\nWidget now keeps a bounded (6-message) conversation history and renders a\nvisible You/Agent transcript, with a \"Start new conversation\" reset button.\nThe server re-validates and re-bounds client-supplied history (sanitizeHistory)\nsince request bodies are untrusted, and still fetches a fresh Orders snapshot\nper request so follow-up questions reason over current state, not a stale\nanswer. Also fixes total-orders currency formatting to use thousands\nseparators and top-aligns the orders-card loading/empty/error state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): Phase 3 host integration — Angular + Vue\n\nWires the shared <business-agent-widget> into Angular and Vue, matching the\nexisting React host adapter (libs/business-agent-react). Neither new host\nlib reimplements any widget behavior — both stay thin registration/config\nwrappers, per docs/roadmap.md Phase 3 constraints.\n\n- libs/business-agent-angular: BusinessAgentPanelComponent, endpoint\n  configured via a BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT InjectionToken (mirrors the\n  existing ORDERS_SOCKET_URL pattern), CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA for the\n  untyped custom element, optional dev-only event logging.\n- libs/business-agent-vue: BusinessAgentPanel.vue, endpoint configured via\n  VITE_BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT (same convention as the React adapter, both\n  being Vite-built), isCustomElement compiler option so Vue doesn''t warn on\n  the unresolved custom element.\n- Both apps get a new UsersPage composition root (mirrors React''s app.tsx)\n  so Users/Orders and the Business Agent stay two independent capabilities\n  routed together, instead of one owning the other.\n- start:angular and start:vue now bundle the WS mock + local business-agent\n  server via concurrently, matching start:react, so the widget actually has\n  something to talk to in local dev.\n- docs/roadmap.md Phase 3 marked implemented.\n\nVerified live in Chrome against the real Claude API on both Angular\n(localhost:4200) and Vue (localhost:4202) — widget renders, and on Angular\na real multi-turn prompt round-tripped through tools/business-agent-server.ts\nwith the correct tool call and answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-portal-vue): add missing global font stylesheet\n\nThe Vue app never had a global stylesheet, so it fell back to the browser''s\ndefault serif font while Angular and React both set Arial/Helvetica sans-serif\nvia their own global styles.css/styles.scss — the three apps looked visually\ninconsistent side by side. Added the same stylesheet Vue was missing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): configurable runtime model + per-query usage telemetry\n\nSwitches the Business Agent''s default model from Opus 4.8 to Sonnet 5,\nconfigurable via BUSINESS_AGENT_MODEL (server-side only, no widget/env\nplumbing). Adds dev-only per-user-query usage/cost telemetry, opt-in via\nBUSINESS_AGENT_USAGE_LOG=1 — aggregates input/output tokens and API call\ncount across every Anthropic call in a run''s tool-use loop (one user\nquestion can drive several calls), never per-call, and logs a single\n[Business Agent Usage] block with an estimated cost from a centralized,\nmodel-keyed pricing table. The public HTTP response contract is unchanged;\nusage stays internal to runAgent''s return value.\n\nAlso fixes an env-loading-order bug caught in review: MODEL and\nORDERS_SNAPSHOT_URL were frozen at module-import time, before loadEnv()\n(which only runs inside the isMain block) had a chance to populate\nprocess.env from the repo''s .env file — so a .env-only\nBUSINESS_AGENT_MODEL or ORDERS_API_URL was silently ignored. Replaced both\nwith functions (getConfiguredModel, getOrdersSnapshotUrl) that read\nprocess.env live on every call; runAgent resolves the model once per run\nand reuses it across all turns.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): Phase 4 — production /api/business-agent endpoint\n\nSplits tools/business-agent-server.ts (previously a single file mixing pure\nagent logic with the local dev HTTP adapter) into a shared core plus two thin\nadapters, and adds the real production serverless handler:\n\n- tools/business-agent-core.ts: all agent logic, framework/transport-agnostic\n  (tools, runAgent, dispatch, sanitizeHistory, fetchOrdersSnapshot, model\n  config, pricing, usage). New here: MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS right-sized to 2048\n  (down from 4096, based on real observed response lengths), AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS\n  (45s, one AbortSignal shared across every turn of a run via the SDK''s\n  actually-supported RequestOptions.signal), SDK_MAX_RETRIES=1 (explicit,\n  documented as distinct from usage.apiCalls/MAX_TURNS), MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH,\n  parseAgentRequestBody/RequestValidationError, and OrdersSnapshotError (a\n  distinguishable class instead of a plain Error, for clean HTTP mapping).\n- tools/business-agent-http.ts: shared, transport-agnostic body-reading (with\n  a 32KB cap, and — found and fixed via live testing, not just unit tests —\n  without destroying the shared req/res socket on overflow) and Content-Type\n  validation (media-type-only, so \"application/json; charset=utf-8\" is\n  accepted).\n- tools/business-agent-errors.ts: maps any error to a stable, sanitized HTTP\n  response. Verified against the actual @anthropic-ai/sdk error class\n  hierarchy (Anthropic.APIError.generate()''s real dispatch), not assumed —\n  the real observed insufficient-credit failure is HTTP 400\n  invalid_request_error (a plain BadRequestError), not a distinct billing\n  error class or a 403, and is covered by a regression test reproducing that\n  exact shape. RateLimitError -> 429, our own timeout\n  (Anthropic.APIUserAbortError) -> 504, Orders backend failure -> 503, every\n  other Anthropic/provider API error -> 503, anything else -> 500. Raw\n  provider text, stack traces, and internal URLs never reach the response.\n- tools/business-agent-server.ts: thinned to the local dev node:http adapter\n  only, unchanged behavior/commands, now importing the shared core.\n- api/business-agent.ts: the production Vercel Node.js Function, at the true\n  monorepo root (confirmed via each project''s dashboard that\n  users-portal-angular/-react/-vue all have Root Directory = ./, so this one\n  file ships identically to all three deployments — genuine same-origin\n  /api/business-agent everywhere, no per-app duplication). Fail-closed CORS\n  (no wildcard fallback in production, unlike the shared dev-only cors.mjs\n  default), strict request validation, sanitized errors, usage logging gated\n  behind BUSINESS_AGENT_USAGE_LOG=1 with no prompt/history logging by\n  default. Plain node:http types, no new @vercel/node dependency.\n\n88 tests across 4 files, all against fake Anthropic/fetch dependencies — zero\nreal API calls in the automated suite. Verified live against both the real\ndev server and a real Node server wrapping the actual production handler\n(not just fakes): successful queries, malformed JSON, oversized\nprompt/body, missing prompt, OPTIONS preflight, and CORS allow/deny origin\nbehavior all confirmed against real HTTP traffic.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(business-agent): resolve Vercel module-resolution failure in api/business-agent.ts\n\nThe real Preview deployment failed at module load with \"Cannot find module\n''../tools/business-agent-core.ts''\" — the explicit .ts extensions on relative\nimports (correct for tools/*.ts files run directly via tsx, whose Node ESM\nloader needs a full specifier) survive untranspiled into Vercel''s compiled\noutput as a literal require(\".../*.ts\"), which Node''s runtime cannot resolve\nin the deployed function (no tsx/ts-node loader active there).\n\nEvery other bundled file in this repo (e.g. libs/business-agent-widget)\nalready uses extensionless relative imports for exactly this reason. Strips\nthe .ts suffix from the only three files that sit at the local-tsx/\nVercel-bundle intersection:\n\n- api/business-agent.ts: imports of ../tools/business-agent-{core,http,errors}\n- tools/business-agent-core.ts: import of ../libs/users/src/index\n- tools/business-agent-errors.ts: imports of ./business-agent-{core,http}\n\ntools/business-agent-server.ts (dev-only, never bundled) and the *.spec.ts\nfiles (vitest tolerates either style) are deliberately untouched.\n\nVerified: 88/88 tests still pass; bundled the fixed api/business-agent.ts\ndirectly with esbuild (same bundler family Vercel''s Node Function builder\nuses) — single self-contained output, all four modules'' logic inlined, zero\nleftover local require() calls; local dev server exercised end-to-end through\nthe new import chain via validation-only requests (no Anthropic call spent).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(railway): include orders store and CORS modules in Docker image\n\ntools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs imports ./orders-store.mjs and ./cors.mjs\n(added when the canonical shared Orders store and CORS handling landed on\nthis branch), but the Dockerfile only ever COPYed the server file itself.\nAny Docker build of this image — Railway production or a feature/PR\nenvironment — would crash at container startup on the unresolved imports.\n\nVerified locally: docker build succeeds, container stays up (no\nmodule-resolution crash), GET /api/orders, GET /api/orders-snapshot, and\nWS /orders all respond correctly, and a WS-emitted order is immediately\nvisible via both HTTP endpoints, confirming the single shared store. No\nRailway config or application logic changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-orders): Angular/Vue Orders source-of-truth parity with React\n\nAngular and Vue served initial Orders from static MOCK_ORDERS while React\nalready read the canonical Railway GET /api/orders — causing the deployed\nUI to disagree with the Business Agent, which reads the live snapshot.\n\nAngular (libs/users-angular/data-access):\n- UserService.getOrdersByUserId now does a real HttpClient GET against a\n  new ORDERS_API_URL injection token (mirrors ORDERS_SOCKET_URL), filtered\n  via the shared getOrdersByUserId util — same pattern React already used.\n- NgRx effects/reducer/selectors/facade needed no changes: the entity\n  adapter''s upsertMany/upsertOne already dedupes by id and already\n  preserves WS orders that arrive before the HTTP load resolves.\n- New Angular \"preview\" build configuration + environment.preview.ts,\n  generated at build time (tools/generate-angular-preview-env.mjs) from\n  Preview-scoped Vercel env vars VITE_ORDERS_API_URL / VITE_ORDERS_WS_URL,\n  gitignored — never a committed constant, since the Railway backend behind\n  it (e.g. a Railway PR Environment) is expected to be ephemeral. Fails\n  clearly (exit 1, no file written) if either var is missing, empty, or\n  whitespace-only. build:prod now branches on $VERCEL_ENV to select the\n  preview vs. production Angular build.\n\nVue (libs/users-vue/data-access, libs/users-vue/feature):\n- orders.api.ts fetches the canonical endpoint (VITE_ORDERS_API_URL,\n  trimmed, falling back to DEFAULT_ORDERS_API_URL) instead of returning\n  filtered MOCK_ORDERS behind an artificial delay. Same trim+fallback\n  convention applied to the WS URL in use-orders-stream.ts.\n- use-orders-stream.ts''s direct WS-append path now dedupes by id (it\n  previously always appended — harmless against frozen mock data, but a\n  real race once both HTTP and WS read the same live store).\n- use-users-facade.ts''s pending-order drain-merge effect (orders buffered\n  before the HTTP load resolves) now merges by id instead of blindly\n  concatenating: collisions between the HTTP snapshot and the pending\n  buffer keep exactly one order with the WS payload winning (freshest\n  observation), pending-only orders are retained, HTTP-snapshot ordering\n  is preserved. pendingOnly is derived from the id-deduped map (not the\n  raw pending array), so two buffered WS updates for the same not-yet-seen\n  id also collapse to one, latest-wins.\n\nTests added: Angular user.service.spec.ts (HttpClient/HttpTestingController)\nand orders.reducer.spec.ts (upsert dedup + WS-before-load survival); Vue\norders.api.spec.ts rewritten, new use-orders-stream.spec.ts, and two new\nuse-users-facade.spec.ts cases covering the full buffer-then-HTTP-collision\nrace and the double-buffered-pending-id race.\n\nReact unchanged — already the reference implementation for this pattern.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(hybrid-mfe): Angular Preview loads the React Preview remote, not production\n\nmain.ts picked the React remoteEntry.js by checking\nwindow.location.hostname === ''localhost'' at runtime — any non-localhost host,\nincluding a Vercel Preview deployment, always fell through to the production\nReact remote. This meant the Angular feat/business-agent Preview''s /hybrid\nroute silently loaded React production instead of its own Preview build, so\nit never showed the new Business Agent work.\n\nFixed using the same build-time environment approach already established for\nOrders: reactRemoteUrl now lives in environment.ts (localhost:4201) and\nenvironment.prod.ts (production remote); main.ts just reads\nenvironment.reactRemoteUrl. tools/generate-angular-preview-env.mjs now also\nreads VITE_REACT_REMOTE_URL (trimmed, same fail-clearly-if-missing/empty/\nwhitespace behavior as the two Orders vars) and writes it into the gitignored,\ngenerated environment.preview.ts — never a committed constant, since the\nReact Preview deployment it points to is tied to a specific branch/PR.\n\nVerified: production build bundles only the production React remote build\n(zero Preview hostname references); Preview build (all three VITE_* vars set)\nbundles all three Preview URLs with zero production references; no\nfeat/business-agent-specific hostname exists anywhere in tracked source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(claude-md): add persistent Claude model-selection policy\n\nSonnet 5 as the default for normal repo work; Opus 5 for genuinely\ndifficult architectural/engineering reasoning; Fable 5 reserved for rare\nwhole-system/adversarial review (report-only by default); Haiku 4.5 for\nlightweight/mechanical work only. Includes the mid-task model-switch\ninteraction protocol and an explicit reminder that a stronger model never\noverrides existing permission/approval rules.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(test): prepare Business Agent for independent review\n\nPhase 5 documentation pass (Steps 1-5) plus Senior Architect self-review\nfixes, prepared as one coherent state for PR #12''s independent review.\n\nDocs:\n- docs/roadmap.md: Phase 4 marked Completed (deployed /api/business-agent,\n  server-side Anthropic credentials, canonical Railway Orders source,\n  HTTP snapshot + WS deltas, Vercel rate limiting, deployed React/Angular/\n  Vue Preview verification, Hybrid Preview verification); Phase 5 marked\n  In Progress\n- docs/business-agent.md (new): architecture, agent loop, source-of-truth\n  model, cross-framework integration, deployment/security, Preview MFE\n  note, grounded example interactions, honest demo-scale simplifications\n- docs/agentic-workflow.md: distinguishes the four development-facing AI\n  surfaces from the product-facing Business Agent, links to the new doc\n- README.md: new Business Agent section; Vue added to the stack table/\n  live-demo badges/summary (previously undocumented despite being live);\n  corrected the canonical-store diagram and prose to show the Business\n  Agent reading GET /api/orders-snapshot (not GET /api/orders, which only\n  frontends use) and softened an overclaimed \"answers can never disagree\"\n  line\n\nSelf-review fixes:\n- Stale \"stays server-side (tools/business-agent-server.ts)\" comments\n  (predating the Phase 4 core/API split) corrected across the widget and\n  all three host adapters, plus a dangling roadmap section-reference\n  comment in api/business-agent.ts\n- Tool descriptions in tools/business-agent-core.ts reworded to state\n  accurately that they operate on the canonical Orders snapshot captured\n  at the start of the current request, not per-tool-call live reads\n  (wording only — the one-snapshot-per-request architecture is unchanged)\n- New tools/business-agent-http.spec.ts: direct boundary/error coverage\n  for readRequestBody/isJsonContentType (exact-byte boundary, multi-chunk\n  overflow, mid-stream errors, settle-once guarantees) that the existing\n  integration tests didn''t isolate\n- New tools/generate-angular-preview-env.spec.ts: child-process coverage\n  of the Angular Preview env generator — success, each required var\n  missing individually and all together, whitespace-only values\n- package.json: registers business-agent-http.spec.ts in test:business-agent\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: harden business agent runtime and architecture guardrails\n\nCorrectness: canonical Orders retention eviction now propagates over WS with\ntombstone-based reconciliation for the WS-before-HTTP hydration race across\nAngular/React/Vue; widget request concurrency/generation hardening; agent-loop\nsnapshot-fetch deadline and MAX_TURNS handling.\n\nArchitecture: enforce framework:* Nx boundaries and wire real ESLint execution\n(incl. Vue SFCs) for React/Vue, previously silently skipped; move generic\nErrorBoundary out of type:ui into app-local per framework; centralize the\nOrderStreamEvent WS contract into @portal/users/utils; retag\nbusiness-agent-widget as type:ui; document the tools/ Business Agent placement\nas a current, reviewed exception; fix PR-review-bot truncation/empty-output\nsafety and make its model configurable; bring CLAUDE.md current (Angular 21,\nVue/Business Agent coverage).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): propagate architecture review failures\n\nnode tools/pr-review-agent.mjs | tee review.md ran under the implicit default\nshell, which is bash -e without pipefail — so a non-zero exit from the Node\nprocess (e.g. the truncated-diff INCOMPLETE case) was masked by tee''s own\nsuccessful exit, and the GitHub check reported success regardless. Explicit\nshell: bash uses Actions'' bash -eo pipefail, so the reviewer''s exit code now\npropagates through the pipe while review.md is still written for the comment\nstep (unchanged, still if: always()).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): allow full architecture review for large PRs\n\nRaises MAX_DIFF_CHARS from 300_000 to 500_000. Verified claude-opus-4-8 (the\npr-review-agent default) has a 1M-token API context window, and this PR''s full\nrequest (diff + CLAUDE.md + rubric) is ~449,654 chars / ~112k tokens — well\nwithin it. 500k remains a deliberate review-policy bound (not a model-context\nworkaround) that still refuses unusually huge PRs; the fail-closed\ntruncated/empty-output behavior in pr-review-format.mjs is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(vue): align business agent panel filename convention\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: address final adversarial review findings\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3697e683c686b73823662036a58cf505f9812575","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Avien/users-portal/git/trees/3697e683c686b73823662036a58cf505f9812575"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Avien/users-portal/git/commits/386ee308025d90866eec09dae52b046a1d169250","verification":{"payload":"tree 3697e683c686b73823662036a58cf505f9812575\nparent 23d73d8ea45237b16488565c2d6434526145c231\nauthor Avi Engelshtein <avi.engelstein@gmail.com> 1787565359 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787565359 +0300\n\nfeat(business-agent): product-facing AI agent (#12)\n\n* fix(business-agent): canonical Orders source-of-truth + move React host adapter to a lib\n\nFixes the Business Agent/UI divergence: the agent read static MOCK_ORDERS\nwhile the UI reflected live WS updates, so the two disagreed about current\nbusiness state. tools/orders-store.mjs is now the single canonical,\nin-memory, cross-connection order store — the origin for every order in\nthe system, including ones generated after process start.\n\n- tools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs: delegates to the store, broadcasts to\n  all WS clients (not just the connecting one), serves GET /api/orders\n  (plain Order[], for frontends) and GET /api/orders-snapshot (one atomic\n  {orders, arrivals} read, agent-only — avoids racing two separate HTTP\n  calls against a new order arriving in between) on the same port/process.\n  Startup demo-burst timers now fire once per process only once they\n  actually run (not once scheduled), fixing a StrictMode interaction where\n  an ephemeral first connection could silently consume the demo burst.\n- tools/business-agent-server.ts: fetchOrdersSnapshot() retries briefly\n  (absorbs the `concurrently` startup race) then fails loudly — no silent\n  fallback to stale static data. getUserOrders/getOrderMonitoringSignals\n  take the snapshot as a parameter instead of a static import.\n  getOrderMonitoringSignals gained real burst detection, reusing\n  isSecondOrderWithinBurstWindow/ORDER_BURST_WINDOW_MS unchanged.\n- tools/cors.mjs (new, shared): wildcard for local dev, a configurable\n  *_ALLOWED_ORIGINS env var for a real production allow-list later.\n- libs/users-react/data-access orders.api.ts: initial load now reads the\n  same canonical GET /api/orders instead of static MOCK_ORDERS, so a\n  browser refresh after WS orders arrived no longer resets to stale data.\n- use-orders-stream.ts / use-users-facade.ts: id-based dedup at both WS\n  merge points — the initial fetch and a WS message can now race and both\n  carry the same order, which the old buffering logic didn''t anticipate.\n- tools/orders-store.mjs: createOrdersStore() factory: one canonical\n  `ordersStore` singleton at runtime, fresh isolated instances in tests\n  (previously mutated shared module state across the suite).\n\nAlso moves the React host adapter for the Business Agent widget out of\nthe app and into libs/business-agent-react (type:feature, framework:react,\nmirrors libs/users-react/feature''s actual config shape) — consistent with\nthis repo''s convention that framework-specific code lives in libs/, not\napps/. The JSX custom-element typing for <business-agent-widget> is\nexposed through the library''s index.ts via a triple-slash reference so\nconsuming apps need no duplicate local .d.ts. The Business Agent panel is\nnow composed directly into the main Users/Orders page (app.tsx is the\ncomposition root; @portal/users-react/feature has no dependency on\n@portal/business-agent-react or vice versa) instead of living behind a\nseparate, undiscoverable /business-agent route.\n\nnpm run start:react now runs the WS mock, Business Agent server, and React\napp together via concurrently, with VITE_BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT wired for\nlocal dev.\n\nVerified end-to-end with the real Claude API and a real browser session:\nUI order count/total update live via WS, and Business Agent answers match\nthat same live state (not stale MOCK_ORDERS) for both a plain\n\"how many orders\" question (searchUsers -> getUserOrders) and a\nmonitoring question (searchUsers -> getOrderMonitoringSignals, correctly\nciting both the high-value and the new burst signal).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-react): restore demo loading latency + stabilize Business Agent layout\n\nTwo UX regressions surfaced by the canonical Orders source change:\n\n1. fetchOrdersByUser lost its ~800ms fake latency when it moved from static\n   MOCK_ORDERS to a real (near-instant) local fetch, so selecting a user no\n   longer visibly demonstrated the query loading state. Restored as optional\n   latency at the HTTP boundary instead of back in the data-access function:\n   tools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs''s GET /api/orders (never /api/orders-snapshot\n   — no reason to slow down the Business Agent) reads MOCK_ORDERS_API_DELAY_MS,\n   defaulting to 0. Railway''s production deployment always gets 0 (railway.json\n   invokes this file directly, bypassing package.json scripts entirely); the\n   800ms demo value is applied only inside start:react''s own mock:ws\n   invocation. TanStack Query caching semantics are untouched — this only\n   changes how long the underlying network response takes.\n\n2. On /users, Business Agent started at the viewport edge instead of the\n   dashboard''s column, and jumped vertically as order counts changed. Root\n   causes and fixes:\n   - Width: UserOrders'' own shellStyle (maxWidth/margin/padding) is now owned\n     by app.tsx''s UsersPage instead — the composition root wraps both\n     UserOrders and BusinessAgentPanel in one flex-column shell, so they share\n     exactly one column instead of each keeping an independently maintained\n     copy of the same constants. UserOrders no longer applies any shell style\n     to its own <section>.\n   - Height: OrdersCard sized its scroll container as\n     Math.min(orders.length, 8) * 52px, so the card visibly grew/shrank with\n     order count, pushing everything below it. Now always renders a fixed\n     416px viewport across all four states (loading/empty/error/list), with\n     the list scrolling inside it past 8 rows exactly as before.\n\nVerified live: dashboard title, Orders card, and Business Agent heading/input\nshare the same left pixel edge (zoomed screenshot); Business Agent''s vertical\nposition stayed identical switching Avi (5 orders) -> Dana (2 orders, through\nthe restored loading state) -> Noam (5 orders); a real Business Agent query\nafterward still answered correctly against the live state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): multi-turn conversation history + widget transcript\n\nWidget now keeps a bounded (6-message) conversation history and renders a\nvisible You/Agent transcript, with a \"Start new conversation\" reset button.\nThe server re-validates and re-bounds client-supplied history (sanitizeHistory)\nsince request bodies are untrusted, and still fetches a fresh Orders snapshot\nper request so follow-up questions reason over current state, not a stale\nanswer. Also fixes total-orders currency formatting to use thousands\nseparators and top-aligns the orders-card loading/empty/error state.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): Phase 3 host integration — Angular + Vue\n\nWires the shared <business-agent-widget> into Angular and Vue, matching the\nexisting React host adapter (libs/business-agent-react). Neither new host\nlib reimplements any widget behavior — both stay thin registration/config\nwrappers, per docs/roadmap.md Phase 3 constraints.\n\n- libs/business-agent-angular: BusinessAgentPanelComponent, endpoint\n  configured via a BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT InjectionToken (mirrors the\n  existing ORDERS_SOCKET_URL pattern), CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA for the\n  untyped custom element, optional dev-only event logging.\n- libs/business-agent-vue: BusinessAgentPanel.vue, endpoint configured via\n  VITE_BUSINESS_AGENT_ENDPOINT (same convention as the React adapter, both\n  being Vite-built), isCustomElement compiler option so Vue doesn''t warn on\n  the unresolved custom element.\n- Both apps get a new UsersPage composition root (mirrors React''s app.tsx)\n  so Users/Orders and the Business Agent stay two independent capabilities\n  routed together, instead of one owning the other.\n- start:angular and start:vue now bundle the WS mock + local business-agent\n  server via concurrently, matching start:react, so the widget actually has\n  something to talk to in local dev.\n- docs/roadmap.md Phase 3 marked implemented.\n\nVerified live in Chrome against the real Claude API on both Angular\n(localhost:4200) and Vue (localhost:4202) — widget renders, and on Angular\na real multi-turn prompt round-tripped through tools/business-agent-server.ts\nwith the correct tool call and answer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-portal-vue): add missing global font stylesheet\n\nThe Vue app never had a global stylesheet, so it fell back to the browser''s\ndefault serif font while Angular and React both set Arial/Helvetica sans-serif\nvia their own global styles.css/styles.scss — the three apps looked visually\ninconsistent side by side. Added the same stylesheet Vue was missing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): configurable runtime model + per-query usage telemetry\n\nSwitches the Business Agent''s default model from Opus 4.8 to Sonnet 5,\nconfigurable via BUSINESS_AGENT_MODEL (server-side only, no widget/env\nplumbing). Adds dev-only per-user-query usage/cost telemetry, opt-in via\nBUSINESS_AGENT_USAGE_LOG=1 — aggregates input/output tokens and API call\ncount across every Anthropic call in a run''s tool-use loop (one user\nquestion can drive several calls), never per-call, and logs a single\n[Business Agent Usage] block with an estimated cost from a centralized,\nmodel-keyed pricing table. The public HTTP response contract is unchanged;\nusage stays internal to runAgent''s return value.\n\nAlso fixes an env-loading-order bug caught in review: MODEL and\nORDERS_SNAPSHOT_URL were frozen at module-import time, before loadEnv()\n(which only runs inside the isMain block) had a chance to populate\nprocess.env from the repo''s .env file — so a .env-only\nBUSINESS_AGENT_MODEL or ORDERS_API_URL was silently ignored. Replaced both\nwith functions (getConfiguredModel, getOrdersSnapshotUrl) that read\nprocess.env live on every call; runAgent resolves the model once per run\nand reuses it across all turns.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(business-agent): Phase 4 — production /api/business-agent endpoint\n\nSplits tools/business-agent-server.ts (previously a single file mixing pure\nagent logic with the local dev HTTP adapter) into a shared core plus two thin\nadapters, and adds the real production serverless handler:\n\n- tools/business-agent-core.ts: all agent logic, framework/transport-agnostic\n  (tools, runAgent, dispatch, sanitizeHistory, fetchOrdersSnapshot, model\n  config, pricing, usage). New here: MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS right-sized to 2048\n  (down from 4096, based on real observed response lengths), AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS\n  (45s, one AbortSignal shared across every turn of a run via the SDK''s\n  actually-supported RequestOptions.signal), SDK_MAX_RETRIES=1 (explicit,\n  documented as distinct from usage.apiCalls/MAX_TURNS), MAX_PROMPT_LENGTH,\n  parseAgentRequestBody/RequestValidationError, and OrdersSnapshotError (a\n  distinguishable class instead of a plain Error, for clean HTTP mapping).\n- tools/business-agent-http.ts: shared, transport-agnostic body-reading (with\n  a 32KB cap, and — found and fixed via live testing, not just unit tests —\n  without destroying the shared req/res socket on overflow) and Content-Type\n  validation (media-type-only, so \"application/json; charset=utf-8\" is\n  accepted).\n- tools/business-agent-errors.ts: maps any error to a stable, sanitized HTTP\n  response. Verified against the actual @anthropic-ai/sdk error class\n  hierarchy (Anthropic.APIError.generate()''s real dispatch), not assumed —\n  the real observed insufficient-credit failure is HTTP 400\n  invalid_request_error (a plain BadRequestError), not a distinct billing\n  error class or a 403, and is covered by a regression test reproducing that\n  exact shape. RateLimitError -> 429, our own timeout\n  (Anthropic.APIUserAbortError) -> 504, Orders backend failure -> 503, every\n  other Anthropic/provider API error -> 503, anything else -> 500. Raw\n  provider text, stack traces, and internal URLs never reach the response.\n- tools/business-agent-server.ts: thinned to the local dev node:http adapter\n  only, unchanged behavior/commands, now importing the shared core.\n- api/business-agent.ts: the production Vercel Node.js Function, at the true\n  monorepo root (confirmed via each project''s dashboard that\n  users-portal-angular/-react/-vue all have Root Directory = ./, so this one\n  file ships identically to all three deployments — genuine same-origin\n  /api/business-agent everywhere, no per-app duplication). Fail-closed CORS\n  (no wildcard fallback in production, unlike the shared dev-only cors.mjs\n  default), strict request validation, sanitized errors, usage logging gated\n  behind BUSINESS_AGENT_USAGE_LOG=1 with no prompt/history logging by\n  default. Plain node:http types, no new @vercel/node dependency.\n\n88 tests across 4 files, all against fake Anthropic/fetch dependencies — zero\nreal API calls in the automated suite. Verified live against both the real\ndev server and a real Node server wrapping the actual production handler\n(not just fakes): successful queries, malformed JSON, oversized\nprompt/body, missing prompt, OPTIONS preflight, and CORS allow/deny origin\nbehavior all confirmed against real HTTP traffic.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(business-agent): resolve Vercel module-resolution failure in api/business-agent.ts\n\nThe real Preview deployment failed at module load with \"Cannot find module\n''../tools/business-agent-core.ts''\" — the explicit .ts extensions on relative\nimports (correct for tools/*.ts files run directly via tsx, whose Node ESM\nloader needs a full specifier) survive untranspiled into Vercel''s compiled\noutput as a literal require(\".../*.ts\"), which Node''s runtime cannot resolve\nin the deployed function (no tsx/ts-node loader active there).\n\nEvery other bundled file in this repo (e.g. libs/business-agent-widget)\nalready uses extensionless relative imports for exactly this reason. Strips\nthe .ts suffix from the only three files that sit at the local-tsx/\nVercel-bundle intersection:\n\n- api/business-agent.ts: imports of ../tools/business-agent-{core,http,errors}\n- tools/business-agent-core.ts: import of ../libs/users/src/index\n- tools/business-agent-errors.ts: imports of ./business-agent-{core,http}\n\ntools/business-agent-server.ts (dev-only, never bundled) and the *.spec.ts\nfiles (vitest tolerates either style) are deliberately untouched.\n\nVerified: 88/88 tests still pass; bundled the fixed api/business-agent.ts\ndirectly with esbuild (same bundler family Vercel''s Node Function builder\nuses) — single self-contained output, all four modules'' logic inlined, zero\nleftover local require() calls; local dev server exercised end-to-end through\nthe new import chain via validation-only requests (no Anthropic call spent).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(railway): include orders store and CORS modules in Docker image\n\ntools/mock-orders-ws-server.mjs imports ./orders-store.mjs and ./cors.mjs\n(added when the canonical shared Orders store and CORS handling landed on\nthis branch), but the Dockerfile only ever COPYed the server file itself.\nAny Docker build of this image — Railway production or a feature/PR\nenvironment — would crash at container startup on the unresolved imports.\n\nVerified locally: docker build succeeds, container stays up (no\nmodule-resolution crash), GET /api/orders, GET /api/orders-snapshot, and\nWS /orders all respond correctly, and a WS-emitted order is immediately\nvisible via both HTTP endpoints, confirming the single shared store. No\nRailway config or application logic changed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(users-orders): Angular/Vue Orders source-of-truth parity with React\n\nAngular and Vue served initial Orders from static MOCK_ORDERS while React\nalready read the canonical Railway GET /api/orders — causing the deployed\nUI to disagree with the Business Agent, which reads the live snapshot.\n\nAngular (libs/users-angular/data-access):\n- UserService.getOrdersByUserId now does a real HttpClient GET against a\n  new ORDERS_API_URL injection token (mirrors ORDERS_SOCKET_URL), filtered\n  via the shared getOrdersByUserId util — same pattern React already used.\n- NgRx effects/reducer/selectors/facade needed no changes: the entity\n  adapter''s upsertMany/upsertOne already dedupes by id and already\n  preserves WS orders that arrive before the HTTP load resolves.\n- New Angular \"preview\" build configuration + environment.preview.ts,\n  generated at build time (tools/generate-angular-preview-env.mjs) from\n  Preview-scoped Vercel env vars VITE_ORDERS_API_URL / VITE_ORDERS_WS_URL,\n  gitignored — never a committed constant, since the Railway backend behind\n  it (e.g. a Railway PR Environment) is expected to be ephemeral. Fails\n  clearly (exit 1, no file written) if either var is missing, empty, or\n  whitespace-only. build:prod now branches on $VERCEL_ENV to select the\n  preview vs. production Angular build.\n\nVue (libs/users-vue/data-access, libs/users-vue/feature):\n- orders.api.ts fetches the canonical endpoint (VITE_ORDERS_API_URL,\n  trimmed, falling back to DEFAULT_ORDERS_API_URL) instead of returning\n  filtered MOCK_ORDERS behind an artificial delay. Same trim+fallback\n  convention applied to the WS URL in use-orders-stream.ts.\n- use-orders-stream.ts''s direct WS-append path now dedupes by id (it\n  previously always appended — harmless against frozen mock data, but a\n  real race once both HTTP and WS read the same live store).\n- use-users-facade.ts''s pending-order drain-merge effect (orders buffered\n  before the HTTP load resolves) now merges by id instead of blindly\n  concatenating: collisions between the HTTP snapshot and the pending\n  buffer keep exactly one order with the WS payload winning (freshest\n  observation), pending-only orders are retained, HTTP-snapshot ordering\n  is preserved. pendingOnly is derived from the id-deduped map (not the\n  raw pending array), so two buffered WS updates for the same not-yet-seen\n  id also collapse to one, latest-wins.\n\nTests added: Angular user.service.spec.ts (HttpClient/HttpTestingController)\nand orders.reducer.spec.ts (upsert dedup + WS-before-load survival); Vue\norders.api.spec.ts rewritten, new use-orders-stream.spec.ts, and two new\nuse-users-facade.spec.ts cases covering the full buffer-then-HTTP-collision\nrace and the double-buffered-pending-id race.\n\nReact unchanged — already the reference implementation for this pattern.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(hybrid-mfe): Angular Preview loads the React Preview remote, not production\n\nmain.ts picked the React remoteEntry.js by checking\nwindow.location.hostname === ''localhost'' at runtime — any non-localhost host,\nincluding a Vercel Preview deployment, always fell through to the production\nReact remote. This meant the Angular feat/business-agent Preview''s /hybrid\nroute silently loaded React production instead of its own Preview build, so\nit never showed the new Business Agent work.\n\nFixed using the same build-time environment approach already established for\nOrders: reactRemoteUrl now lives in environment.ts (localhost:4201) and\nenvironment.prod.ts (production remote); main.ts just reads\nenvironment.reactRemoteUrl. tools/generate-angular-preview-env.mjs now also\nreads VITE_REACT_REMOTE_URL (trimmed, same fail-clearly-if-missing/empty/\nwhitespace behavior as the two Orders vars) and writes it into the gitignored,\ngenerated environment.preview.ts — never a committed constant, since the\nReact Preview deployment it points to is tied to a specific branch/PR.\n\nVerified: production build bundles only the production React remote build\n(zero Preview hostname references); Preview build (all three VITE_* vars set)\nbundles all three Preview URLs with zero production references; no\nfeat/business-agent-specific hostname exists anywhere in tracked source.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(claude-md): add persistent Claude model-selection policy\n\nSonnet 5 as the default for normal repo work; Opus 5 for genuinely\ndifficult architectural/engineering reasoning; Fable 5 reserved for rare\nwhole-system/adversarial review (report-only by default); Haiku 4.5 for\nlightweight/mechanical work only. Includes the mid-task model-switch\ninteraction protocol and an explicit reminder that a stronger model never\noverrides existing permission/approval rules.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* docs(test): prepare Business Agent for independent review\n\nPhase 5 documentation pass (Steps 1-5) plus Senior Architect self-review\nfixes, prepared as one coherent state for PR #12''s independent review.\n\nDocs:\n- docs/roadmap.md: Phase 4 marked Completed (deployed /api/business-agent,\n  server-side Anthropic credentials, canonical Railway Orders source,\n  HTTP snapshot + WS deltas, Vercel rate limiting, deployed React/Angular/\n  Vue Preview verification, Hybrid Preview verification); Phase 5 marked\n  In Progress\n- docs/business-agent.md (new): architecture, agent loop, source-of-truth\n  model, cross-framework integration, deployment/security, Preview MFE\n  note, grounded example interactions, honest demo-scale simplifications\n- docs/agentic-workflow.md: distinguishes the four development-facing AI\n  surfaces from the product-facing Business Agent, links to the new doc\n- README.md: new Business Agent section; Vue added to the stack table/\n  live-demo badges/summary (previously undocumented despite being live);\n  corrected the canonical-store diagram and prose to show the Business\n  Agent reading GET /api/orders-snapshot (not GET /api/orders, which only\n  frontends use) and softened an overclaimed \"answers can never disagree\"\n  line\n\nSelf-review fixes:\n- Stale \"stays server-side (tools/business-agent-server.ts)\" comments\n  (predating the Phase 4 core/API split) corrected across the widget and\n  all three host adapters, plus a dangling roadmap section-reference\n  comment in api/business-agent.ts\n- Tool descriptions in tools/business-agent-core.ts reworded to state\n  accurately that they operate on the canonical Orders snapshot captured\n  at the start of the current request, not per-tool-call live reads\n  (wording only — the one-snapshot-per-request architecture is unchanged)\n- New tools/business-agent-http.spec.ts: direct boundary/error coverage\n  for readRequestBody/isJsonContentType (exact-byte boundary, multi-chunk\n  overflow, mid-stream errors, settle-once guarantees) that the existing\n  integration tests didn''t isolate\n- New tools/generate-angular-preview-env.spec.ts: child-process coverage\n  of the Angular Preview env generator — success, each required var\n  missing individually and all together, whitespace-only values\n- package.json: registers business-agent-http.spec.ts in test:business-agent\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: harden business agent runtime and architecture guardrails\n\nCorrectness: canonical Orders retention eviction now propagates over WS with\ntombstone-based reconciliation for the WS-before-HTTP hydration race across\nAngular/React/Vue; widget request concurrency/generation hardening; agent-loop\nsnapshot-fetch deadline and MAX_TURNS handling.\n\nArchitecture: enforce framework:* Nx boundaries and wire real ESLint execution\n(incl. Vue SFCs) for React/Vue, previously silently skipped; move generic\nErrorBoundary out of type:ui into app-local per framework; centralize the\nOrderStreamEvent WS contract into @portal/users/utils; retag\nbusiness-agent-widget as type:ui; document the tools/ Business Agent placement\nas a current, reviewed exception; fix PR-review-bot truncation/empty-output\nsafety and make its model configurable; bring CLAUDE.md current (Angular 21,\nVue/Business Agent coverage).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): propagate architecture review failures\n\nnode tools/pr-review-agent.mjs | tee review.md ran under the implicit default\nshell, which is bash -e without pipefail — so a non-zero exit from the Node\nprocess (e.g. the truncated-diff INCOMPLETE case) was masked by tee''s own\nsuccessful exit, and the GitHub check reported success regardless. Explicit\nshell: bash uses Actions'' bash -eo pipefail, so the reviewer''s exit code now\npropagates through the pipe while review.md is still written for the comment\nstep (unchanged, still if: always()).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(ci): allow full architecture review for large PRs\n\nRaises MAX_DIFF_CHARS from 300_000 to 500_000. Verified claude-opus-4-8 (the\npr-review-agent default) has a 1M-token API context window, and this PR''s full\nrequest (diff + CLAUDE.md + rubric) is ~449,654 chars / ~112k tokens — well\nwithin it. 500k remains a deliberate review-policy bound (not a model-context\nworkaround) that still refuses unusually huge PRs; the fail-closed\ntruncated/empty-output behavior in pr-review-format.mjs is unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(vue): align business agent panel filename convention\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix: address final adversarial review findings\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqjBUvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAWj4QAIC0P8qiRDTcr/LMm44iLD4/\nJz4gKyKhwFrBCrVZeOgmODVYEB14KVfuEe9649VKGKl7PtE2VSDydUAlT1UsXCKs\nQ1dJGP/Swpz/hixjKUDWMLJjNWksAdXpUj11fZedqTX1Z3qtZ6rJadwcYN9cuNPv\n0/rZhYud2ldoTMyGALxk6Zo5Bii521LPxc3X0tl3BBToUzyjWIpR7/ZE76vGmX/I\njY9/YPUXSOBPm+6Hvg3eYG/0nEABdxub0RIFAfmiboTku9BWt88TaDDUNMt5j7YR\nyntn4BqT444tRq5gqUhR3Fr8PgH0CWYEqw7v08bGGMyIHwUk4Io2uLYvBJNWPkVy\nkqWGm9z9RChIGp/LLCCMNc2knOCKi8+68pnuNE1SUQTSSZkF8eFElR6i